== Roleplaying Background

The Old World is dying. From the north comes a tide of corruption that devours everything it touches. The Empire's borders have collapsed. The Dwarf Holds are silent. Even the Elves have abandoned their shores.

You are what remains. A warlord of a fractured realm, commanding the last loyal soldiers in a world that has forgotten hope. Your fortress is the only bastion of order for a hundred leagues. Every day brings new threats — not just from the armies of Chaos, but from within. Resources dwindle. The gods have abandoned you.

But you did not come this far to die.

== Wave 2 Guide — The End Times

== Campaign Philosophy

Wave 2 introduces SFO: Grimhammer III and the mechanics mods built around it. These fundamentally change how you play. Your approach must evolve:

- *SFO pacing is the new normal.* Battles last longer, XP comes slower, and every unit has a defined role. SFO rebalances the entire game around its lore-flavoured vision — expect to relearn matchups you thought you knew.
- *Winning is a journey, not a checklist.* Victory Conditions Overhaul replaces short/long objectives with three thematic routes per faction (50–70 turns), each with unique rewards, quest battles and epilogues. Decide early which route you are pursuing — objectives are set on turn 1.
- *Armies must be built, not stacked.* Tabletop Caps limit Core/Special/Rare units per army for the player AND the AI. Doomstacks are gone; balanced, lore-like armies are the standard.
- *The AI plays smarter.* AI Recruitment & Army Compositions and the Siege AI Overhaul mean enemy armies are balanced and sieges are fought, not attrition-starved.

== Core Mechanics Changes

=== SFO Combat Pacing
Battles are longer and more decisive. Units are slower to break and harder to replace.
- Protect your elite units — SFO makes losses painful and recovery expensive.
- Use terrain and flanking; brute-force trades favour the AI's numbers.

=== Victory Routes
Each faction has 3 thematic victory routes with unique rewards and quest battles.
- Choose your route by turn 1 and commit.
- Route rewards include custom units and epilogues — read the VCO objectives panel regularly.

=== Army Composition Limits
Tabletop-style caps force balanced armies for both sides.
- Plan rosters around Core (reliable), Special (situational) and Rare (decisive) slots.
- The AI operates under the same limits — exploit that symmetry.

=== Smarter AI
Enemy armies are themed around their legendary lord and sieges are aggressive.
- Expect AI armies with proper line infantry and capped monsters, not spam.
- Defend walls and gates — Siege AI rework makes towers and attrition meaningful.

== Difficulty Curve

Wave 2 is designed to be punishing. Key challenges:
- Longer, more tactical battles under SFO
- AI that builds balanced, thematic armies
- Aggressive siege AI that attacks instead of waiting
- Victory routes that demand focused, long-term planning

== Recommended Progression

- Turns 1-10: Consolidate your fortress. Pick your VCO route. Build toward the army cap structure you want.
- Turns 11-25: Expand carefully. Take only provinces you can defend against a smarter AI.
- Turns 26+: Begin offensive operations. Strike at weakened enemies and pursue your victory route.

== Prerequisites Before Wave 3

You should be comfortable with:
- Managing SFO's longer battles and slower progression
- Building armies under Tabletop Caps
- Pursuing a VCO victory route
- Beating a balanced, aggressive AI

== Important

Start a new campaign when activating Wave 2. SFO, VCO and Tabletop Caps require it — do not continue a Wave 1 save. Install the VCO-SFO compatibility patch and the SFO patches for any Wave 1 content mods you use (see the content and mechanics sections).
